    defaultFieldsJson: {
      total_phases: 6,
      phases: [
        'problem_definition', 'data_acquisition', 'model_development',
        'pre_deployment_validation', 'deployment', 'production_monitoring'
      ],
      body_text: 'AI/ML Model Lifecycle Governance — Six-Phase Framework\n\nThe Model Lifecycle Governance label defines a six-phase framework that every AI/ML model must traverse from inception to decommissioning. Each phase has mandatory artifacts, a named approver, exit criteria, and audit retention requirements. The framework is aligned with the EU AI Act Article 15 (accuracy, robustness, cybersecurity), the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage), and the U.S. Federal Reserve SR 11-7 Guidance on Model Risk Management. No model may skip a phase or proceed without the approver sign-off recorded in the Model Inventory.\n\nPhase 1 — Problem Definition. The use case is documented in a Model Charter, including the business problem, the decision the model will inform or automate, the downstream consumer, the materiality tier (Critical/High/Medium/Low per the AI/ML Model Risk Tier label), the protected classes implicated, and the expected deployment context. A Preliminary Risk Assessment identifies foreseeable misuses, adversarial attack surfaces, and the failure mode severity. Approver: Model Risk Committee for Critical and High tiers; Senior Compliance Officer for Medium and Low. Exit criteria: signed Model Charter, Preliminary Risk Assessment, and materiality tier classification.\n\nPhase 2 — Data Acquisition. The training, validation, and test datasets are sourced, lineage-traced, and licensed. A Data Sheet (per Gebru et al., 2018) records the dataset provenance, collection method, intended use, known biases, and consent basis. Special category data under GDPR Article 9 or PIPL Article 28 triggers a mandatory Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) per Article 35 GDPR. Data minimisation is enforced: only fields materially relevant to the problem may be retained. Synthetic data may be used as a supplement but cannot be the sole training source for Critical-tier models. Approver: Data Protection Officer plus the named model owner. Exit criteria: signed Data Sheet, completed DPIA (if triggered), data lineage graph, and license/consent attestation.\n\nPhase 3 — Model Development. The model architecture, training pipeline, hyperparameter selection rationale, and feature engineering are documented in a Model Card (per Mitchell et al., 2019). The development environment is isolated from production data stores. Versioning is mandatory: every training run produces a versioned artifact in the Model Registry with a content hash, training data snapshot hash, code commit hash, and hyperparameter manifest. Reproducibility is enforced: the same code + data + hyperparameters must produce the same model within a tolerance of 1e-6. Fairness metrics are computed across all protected classes during development, not deferred to validation. Approver: Model owner with peer review by a second data scientist. Exit criteria: Model Card, versioned artifact, fairness metric report, and peer review sign-off.\n\nPhase 4 — Pre-Deployment Validation. Independent validation by the second line of defence (Model Validation team). The validator recomputes performance metrics on a held-out test set the development team has not seen, runs the fairness metric suite, performs stress tests (data perturbation, adversarial inputs, distribution shift), and reviews the Model Card for completeness. A Challenger Model is required for Critical-tier models: a simpler, interpretable baseline must be trained and compared; if the challenger achieves within 5% of the proposed model performance, the simpler model is preferred. Validation findings are categorised as No Issue, Observation, Recommendation, or Required Remediation; only No Issue or cleared Required Remediation permits progression. Approver: Head of Model Validation (independent of the development team). Exit criteria: signed Validation Report, cleared remediation items, Challenger Model comparison, and stress test results.\n\nPhase 5 — Deployment. The model is deployed to production behind a feature flag with a defined rollback trigger. A Decision Rights Matrix specifies which decisions the model may make autonomously, which require human concurrence, and which require human affirmative approval (per the tier override requirements). A Pre-Mortem is conducted: the team enumerates the ways the deployment could fail and the rollback procedures for each. The deployment is staged: canary (5% traffic), ramp (25%), and full (100%), with a 72-hour hold at each stage to surface delayed failure modes. Approver: Change Advisory Board plus the tier-appropriate authority (Board for Critical, CEO/CRO for High). Exit criteria: signed Decision Rights Matrix, Pre-Mortem document, deployment runbook, and staged rollout sign-off.\n\nPhase 6 — Production Monitoring. The model is monitored continuously for input drift (Population Stability Index), output drift (prediction distribution shift), performance drift (AUC/precision/recall degradation), and fairness drift (DIR falling below 0.80 on any protected class). Monitoring dashboards are reviewed weekly by the model owner and monthly by the Model Risk Committee for Critical-tier models. A Drift Threshold Breach triggers automatic quarantine: the model is reverted to the last known good version within 24 hours pending investigation. Annual re-validation is mandatory for Critical-tier models; bi-annual for High; every 18 months for Medium; every 24 months for Low. Decommissioning requires a Decommissioning Record documenting the rationale, the data retention plan, and the successor model (if any). Approver: Model Risk Committee (annual review); Model owner (operational monitoring). Exit criteria: live monitoring dashboards, drift breach runbook, annual re-validation record, and (at end of life) Decommissioning Record.\n\nThe lifecycle framework integrates with the AI/ML Model Risk Tier label (tier drives approver levels and audit cadence), the Explainability Requirements label (Tier 1/2 require SHAP or counterfactual in Phase 3), and the Fairness Metric Matrix label (Phase 3/4 require the full metric suite). It also feeds the XCC (Explainable Compliance Card) view so that every model-driven decision carries a compliance card recording the current phase, the last approver, and the next review date.',
    },
