      body_text: 'FATF Travel Rule Form (Recommendation 16) — Virtual Asset Transfers\n\nThe FATF Recommendation 16 Travel Rule extends the traditional wire-transfer travel rule to transfers of virtual assets. The form operationalises the FATF Travel Rule Guidance (October 2021 update) for VASPs (Virtual Asset Service Providers), requiring the originating VASP to transmit originator and beneficiary information to the beneficiary VASP concurrently with the virtual asset transfer, and the beneficiary VASP to make this information available to the relevant authorities on request. The form supports the OpenVASPA (Open Virtual Asset Service Provider API) protocol, an open standard developed by the IVMS101 working group for VASP-to-VASP information exchange, and is compatible with the TRP (Travel Rule Protocol), Sygna Bridge, and Sumsub implementations.\n\nThreshold applicability. The Travel Rule applies to all virtual asset transfers above USD/EUR 1,000 for cross-border transfers and for domestic transfers in jurisdictions that have implemented the domestic threshold (the U.S. BSA travel rule threshold for virtual assets is USD 3,000, set by FinCEN in the 2024 NRF Travel Rule final rule). Below-threshold transfers require only the originator and beneficiary wallet addresses, without identifying information, but the VASP must still monitor for structuring patterns of sub-threshold transfers designed to evade the travel rule.\n\nOriginator information (mandatory above threshold). The form requires: (a) originator name (the natural or legal person initiating the transfer); (b) originator account number (the wallet address or internal ledger account identifier); (c) originator physical address (street, city, country); (d) originator national identity number (e.g. passport number, national ID number, or LEI for legal persons); and (e) originator date of birth (for natural persons). The FATF Guidance permits a layered approach: the originating VASP must collect all five fields from the originator, but may transmit only the name, account number, and one of (physical address, national ID, or DOB) to the beneficiary VASP, with the remaining fields available on request from the competent authority.\n\nBeneficiary information (mandatory above threshold). The form requires: (a) beneficiary name (the natural or legal person receiving the transfer); and (b) beneficiary account number (the wallet address or internal ledger account identifier). The originating VASP must verify the beneficiary name against the wallet address via a pre-transfer check with the beneficiary VASP (using the OpenVASPA transfer_pre_check message) before executing the transfer. Failed pre-checks (name mismatch, wallet not held at the named beneficiary VASP, beneficiary VASP sanctioned) trigger a hold-and-investigate workflow.\n\nOpenVASPA protocol message structure. The form encodes the data into an OpenVASPA payload with five message types: (1) transfer_pre_check — sent before execution to verify the beneficiary wallet and name; (2) transfer_request — sent concurrently with the on-chain transfer, carrying the full originator and beneficiary information; (3) transfer_confirmation — sent by the beneficiary VASP upon receipt of the on-chain funds, confirming successful transfer; (4) transfer_cancellation — sent by either VASP to cancel a pending transfer (with reason code); and (5) transfer_beneficiary_confirmation — sent by the beneficiary VASP to confirm the beneficiary has been identified. Each message carries a unique openvaspa_message_id (UUID v4), the openvaspa_payload_version, a signature from the sending VASP, and a timestamp.\n\nIVMS101 data model. The originator and beneficiary information is structured per the IVMS101 (InterVASP Messaging Standard 101) data model, which defines a common JSON schema for natural persons (name as a structured object with given_name, name_prefix, family_name), legal persons (name as a string, geographic_addresses array, national_identification object with identifier_type and identifier), and account numbers (with the address_type field distinguishing blockchain addresses from internal ledger accounts). The IVMS101 schema is mandatory for OpenVASPA-compliant implementations and is the lingua franca for cross-protocol interoperability (TRP, Sygna, Sumsub all map to IVMS101).\n\nSunrise period handling. The FATF Guidance acknowledges that not all jurisdictions have implemented the Travel Rule at the same pace, creating a "sunrise period" during which VASPs in Travel Rule jurisdictions may need to transact with VASPs in non-Travel Rule jurisdictions. The form supports three sunrise period strategies: (1) refuse the transfer (the conservative approach, recommended for transfers above USD/EUR 1,000); (2) accept the transfer with enhanced due diligence on the counterparty VASP (the intermediate approach, requiring a risk-based assessment and MLRO approval); and (3) accept the transfer with a documented risk acceptance (the permissive approach, only for low-risk transfers below USD/EUR 1,000 with a documented counterparty VASP due diligence file). Each sunrise period transfer is logged with the strategy applied, the approving officer, and the counterparty VASP risk assessment.\n\nCross-jurisdictional differences. The form accommodates the divergent implementation timelines: the EU implemented the Travel Rule via the TFR (Transfer of Funds Regulation) Amendment, effective December 30, 2024, with a EUR 1,000 threshold; the U.S. implemented via the FinCEN NRF Travel Rule final rule, effective May 2025, with a USD 3,000 threshold; Singapore implemented via the MAS PSN02 Notice, effective January 2022, with a SGD 1,500 threshold; Hong Kong implemented via the HKMA AMLO Schedule 2 amendment, effective June 2023, with a HKD 8,000 threshold; and the UK implemented via the MLR 2017 amendment, effective September 2023, with a GBP 1,000 threshold. The form auto-selects the applicable threshold based on the originator and beneficiary VASP jurisdictions.\n\nRecordkeeping. Originating and beneficiary VASPs must retain the Travel Rule information for 5 years (FATF Recommendation 11), with the records accessible to competent authorities within 24 hours of request. The records include the OpenVASPA payload, the on-chain transaction hash, the pre-check response, and any sunrise period risk acceptance documentation.\n\nThe form integrates with the MiCA CASP authorization form (Article 49 of MiCA imposes an equivalent travel rule obligation on CASPs) and with the Sanctions Screening view so that the originator and beneficiary wallet addresses are screened against OFAC, EU CFSP, and UN sanctions lists at the pre-check stage, with sanctioned wallets triggering an automatic block and SAR filing.',
