    defaultFieldsJson: {
      total_frameworks: 3,
      frameworks: ['issb_s1_s2', 'esrs', 'sec_climate'],
      body_text: 'ESG Framework Comparison — ISSB IFRS S1/S2 vs ESRS vs SEC Climate\n\nThe ESG Framework Comparison label provides a side-by-side reference for the three leading sustainability disclosure frameworks applicable to financial institutions: the ISSB IFRS S1 (General Requirements) and S2 (Climate-related Disclosures), the ESRS (European Sustainability Reporting Standards) under the CSRD (Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive), and the SEC Climate Rule (Final Rule, March 2024). The three frameworks diverge on materiality basis, scope, assurance, and digital taxonomy; a financial institution operating across the EU, U.S., and global capital markets must satisfy all three, with the ESRS generally being the most demanding. The comparison is structured around seven dimensions: materiality basis, scope, scope 3 emissions, assurance level, effective date, digital taxonomy, and forward-looking scenario analysis.\n\nDimension 1 — Materiality basis. ISSB S1/S2 uses a financial materiality basis (sustainability information that is material to the enterprise value of the reporting entity), aligned with the TCFD recommendations and the IASB Conceptual Framework. ESRS uses a double materiality basis: financial (or "financial-to-enterprise-value") materiality plus impact (or "inside-out") materiality, requiring the entity to report both how sustainability matters affect the entity and how the entity affects sustainability matters. SEC Climate Rule uses a financial materiality basis (consistent with Reg S-K Item 303) for the qualitative disclosures and a reasonable likelihood threshold for the financial impact disclosures. The double materiality basis of ESRS is the most expansive and captures information that the ISSB and SEC frameworks would not require.\n\nDimension 2 — Scope. ISSB S1/S2 applies to all entities required to apply IFRS Accounting Standards, with a phased-on approach for SMEs. ESRS applies to all large EU companies (over 250 employees and EUR 50M turnover or EUR 25M balance sheet) and all listed SMEs from 2026, plus non-EU companies with EU turnover above EUR 150M (with a EU branch or subsidiary) from 2028 (the so-called "CSRD wave 3"). SEC Climate Rule applies to all SEC registrants (domestic and foreign private issuers), with phased compliance based on filer status (LAF, AF, SRC, EGC). The ESRS scope is the broadest, capturing approximately 50,000 EU companies plus 10,000+ non-EU companies; the SEC scope captures approximately 4,000 U.S. registrants plus 1,000 foreign private issuers; the ISSB scope is jurisdiction-dependent (each adopting jurisdiction defines the mandatory scope).\n\nDimension 3 — Scope 3 emissions. ISSB S2 requires Scope 3 greenhouse gas emissions disclosure for all material categories, with a one-year deferral from the initial application date. ESRS E1 requires Scope 3 disclosure for all material categories from year one, with no deferral. SEC Climate Rule requires Scope 3 disclosure only for accelerated filers and only if the entity has set a Scope 3 emissions target, with the disclosure subject to a 10% safe harbour for the first three years. The ESRS is the most demanding on Scope 3; the SEC is the least demanding (with the target-triggered approach criticised by climate advocacy groups as a loophole).\n\nDimension 4 — Assurance level. ISSB S1/S2 does not mandate a specific assurance level, leaving it to adopting jurisdictions (the U.K. is consulting on limited assurance from 2025; Japan is consulting on limited assurance from 2027). ESRS requires limited assurance from 2028, with a transition to reasonable assurance subject to a future Commission decision (no earlier than 2028). SEC Climate Rule does not mandate assurance for the qualitative disclosures but requires the Scope 1 and 2 emissions disclosures (for LAF and AF filers) to be subject to limited assurance for fiscal years 2029-2031 and reasonable assurance from 2033. The ESRS assurance timeline is the most accelerated; the SEC timeline is the longest (with reasonable assurance only from 2033).\n\nDimension 5 — Effective dates. ISSB S1/S2 is effective for annual periods beginning on or after January 1, 2024, for entities that have already adopted IFRS Accounting Standards; the first annual reports under ISSB S1/S2 are being published in 2025. ESRS is phased: FY 2024 for the largest EU companies (over 500 employees already subject to NFRD); FY 2025 for other large EU companies; FY 2026 for listed SMEs (with a two-year opt-out); FY 2028 for non-EU companies meeting the turnover threshold. SEC Climate Rule is phased: FY 2024 for LAF (Scope 1+2 financial impact, governance, risk management); FY 2025 for AF; FY 2026 for SRC and EGC (voluntary) and FY 2027 for SRC/EGC (mandatory if material); Scope 1+2 emissions subject to assurance from FY 2029 (LAF), FY 2030 (AF).\n\nDimension 6 — Digital taxonomy. ISSB S1/S2 uses the IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Taxonomy (based on XBRL), published in February 2024 as a draft for public consultation; the final taxonomy is expected in Q4 2024. ESRS uses the ESRS XBRL Taxonomy, developed by EFRAG and published in August 2024; the taxonomy is mandatory for tagged digital filings from 2028. SEC Climate Rule uses inline XBRL for the structured emissions disclosures (Scope 1+2 for LAF and AF filers), with tagging required from FY 2026; the SEC has not yet issued a climate-specific taxonomy but is using structured data concepts from the existing Reg S-K and Reg S-X taxonomies. All three frameworks are converging on XBRL as the digital format, but the taxonomies are not yet interoperable (a mapping exercise is required for entities filing under multiple frameworks).\n\nDimension 7 — Forward-looking scenario analysis. ISSB S2 requires scenario analysis using a range of climate-related scenarios, including at least one scenario consistent with limiting global warming to 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels (the Paris Agreement goal). ESRS E1 requires scenario analysis using the IEA (International Energy Agency) scenarios, including the Net Zero Emissions by 2050 (NZE) scenario, the Stated Policies (STEPS) scenario, and the Announced Pledges (AP) scenario. SEC Climate Rule requires scenario analysis only for the financial impact disclosures, with a reasonable likelihood threshold that effectively requires only a single most-likely scenario (a weakness compared to ISSB and ESRS). The ISSB and ESRS approaches are aligned with the NGFS (Network for Greening the Financial System) climate scenarios used by central banks for stress testing.\n\nCross-framework compliance strategy. A financial institution operating across the EU, U.S., and global capital markets should: (a) establish a single sustainability data architecture covering Scope 1, 2, and 3 emissions, climate risk, and social/governance data, with the data point taxonomy mapped to all three frameworks; (b) prepare the ESRS-compliant report as the "long-form" disclosure (the most demanding), and extract the ISSB-equivalent and SEC-equivalent disclosures via a mapping crosswalk; (c) maintain a "frameworks differences log" documenting where the ESRS disclosure exceeds the ISSB or SEC requirements and where the SEC or ISSB disclosure omits ESRS-required information; and (d) seek limited assurance from year one (anticipating the ESRS 2028 deadline and the SEC 2029 deadline for LAF).\n\nThe label integrates with the Climate & ESG view (PCAF financed emissions) so that the Scope 3 financed emissions data is sourced from the PCAF calculation and disclosed under all three frameworks, and with the Counterfactual Simulator view so that the climate scenario analysis can be run on demand for any of the NGFS pathways.',
    },
    tags: ['ESG', 'ISSB', 'IFRS-S1', 'IFRS-S2', 'ESRS', 'CSRD', 'SEC', 'climate', 'disclosure'],
    enabledByDefault: true,
    defaultForCategory: false,
