    defaultFieldsJson: {
      total_categories: 4,
      categories: ['algorithmic_fairness', 'customer_outcome_fairness', 'workforce_diversity', 'community_investment'],
      body_text: 'ESG Social Capital Metrics — Algorithmic Fairness as an ESG Metric\n\nThe ESG Social Capital Metrics label defines a four-category framework for social capital disclosure, with the distinctive innovation that algorithmic fairness is treated as a first-class ESG metric alongside the traditional customer outcome fairness, workforce diversity, and community investment categories. The framework addresses the historical gap in ESG social metrics, which have been dominated by workforce diversity and philanthropy at the expense of measuring whether the financial institution actually treats its customers fairly through its automated decisioning systems. The framework is aligned with the ESRS S1 (Social Standards under CSRD), ISSB S1 (General Requirements), GRI 401-403 (Employment, Labor/Management Relations, Occupational Health and Safety), and the U.K. FCA Consumer Duty (Principles 7 and 12).\n\nCategory 1 — Algorithmic Fairness as ESG. Algorithmic fairness is the social capital metric measuring whether the institution AI/ML decisioning systems produce equitable outcomes across protected classes. The category is the most innovative of the four and the most directly tied to financial sector risk: an institution with biased AI models faces both regulatory risk (ECOA, FHA, EU AI Act Article 10) and reputational risk (consumer advocacy campaigns, social media scrutiny). The required disclosures are: (a) the count of AI/ML models in production by tier (Critical/High/Medium/Low per the AI/ML Model Risk Tier label); (b) the count of models with bias testing completed in the reporting period, broken down by tier; (c) the disparate impact ratio (DIR) for each Critical-tier model, with the EEOC 4/5ths threshold of 0.80 highlighted; (d) the count of models failing the DIR threshold, the remediation status, and the interim mitigations applied; (e) the count of customer complaints related to algorithmic decisions (e.g. "why was I declined for credit"), broken down by product and protected class; and (f) the count of human override events for adverse decisions, broken down by product. The data source is the Model Inventory (for model counts and DIR) and the Complaints Register (for complaint data). Disclosure is mandatory under ESRS S1 (paragraph 60) for large EU entities and is recommended under ISSB S1 for entities with material algorithmic-decision risk.\n\nCategory 2 — Customer Outcome Fairness (Consumer Duty). Customer outcome fairness measures whether the institution products and services produce good outcomes for customers, particularly vulnerable customers. The required disclosures are: (a) the count of products subject to the FCA Consumer Duty Outcome monitoring (four outcomes: products and services, price and value, consumer understanding, consumer support); (b) the count of products with a fair value assessment completed in the reporting period; (c) the count of customers identified as vulnerable (per the FCA FG21/1 guidance on the fair treatment of vulnerable customers); (d) the count of customer complaints by outcome category, with the vulnerable customer share highlighted; (e) the count of product withdrawals or modifications following outcome monitoring; and (f) the count of customer redress payments (refunds, compensation) by product, with the total amount paid. The data source is the Consumer Duty Monitoring Register and the Complaints Register. Disclosure is mandatory under ESRS S1 (paragraphs 50-55) and is required for FCA-regulated firms under the Consumer Duty (in force from July 31, 2023).\n\nCategory 3 — Workforce Diversity & Inclusion. Workforce diversity measures the demographic composition of the institution workforce at all levels, with particular attention to senior management and board representation. The required disclosures are: (a) the gender split of the workforce, broken down by seniority band (board, executive committee, senior management, middle management, junior, overall); (b) the ethnic minority representation in the U.K. and U.S. workforce, broken down by seniority band; (c) the age distribution of the workforce (16-24, 25-34, 35-44, 45-54, 55-64, 65+); (d) the disability representation, with the count of reasonable adjustments made; (e) the LGBTQ+ representation (where voluntarily disclosed); (f) the gender pay gap (U.K. mandatory) and the ethnicity pay gap (U.K. voluntary, but recommended); (g) the count of pay equity audits completed and the count of pay adjustments made; and (h) the count of discrimination complaints filed with HR, with the resolution status. The data source is the HR Information System. Disclosure is mandatory under ESRS S1 (paragraphs 19-25) and the U.K. Gender Pay Gap Information Regulations 2017; ethnic minority disclosure is recommended under ISSB S1 and is mandatory under the Parker Review for U.K. FTSE 350 companies.\n\nCategory 4 — Community Investment. Community investment measures the institution contribution to the communities in which it operates, including philanthropic donations, financial inclusion programs, and infrastructure investment in underserved areas. The required disclosures are: (a) the total community investment spend in the reporting period, broken down by category (philanthropy, financial inclusion, infrastructure, education, environment); (b) the count of financial inclusion products offered (basic bank accounts, microfinance, low-fee remittances); (c) the count of customers using financial inclusion products; (d) the count of bank branches in low-income or underserved areas, with the year-on-year change (addressing the "banking desert" issue); (e) the count of community development financial institution (CDFI) partnerships; and (f) the count of employees volunteering in community programs, with the total hours. The data source is the Community Investment Register. Disclosure is mandatory under ESRS S1 (paragraphs 70-75) and is recommended under ISSB S1.\n\nCross-category integration. The four categories are integrated through the "fairness through the customer journey" lens: algorithmic fairness (Category 1) determines whether customers are offered products equitably; customer outcome fairness (Category 2) determines whether the offered products produce good outcomes; workforce diversity (Category 3) determines whether the institution has the diverse perspectives necessary to design fair products; and community investment (Category 4) determines whether the institution is reaching underserved communities. A material weakness in any category indicates a fairness gap that the institution should address.\n\nMateriality thresholds. Algorithmic fairness (Category 1) is material for any institution with more than 5 Critical-tier models or with more than 100,000 algorithmic decisions per year affecting retail customers. Customer outcome fairness (Category 2) is material for any retail-facing institution. Workforce diversity (Category 3) is material for any institution with more than 250 employees (the ESRS threshold). Community investment (Category 4) is material for any institution with total assets above USD 50 billion or with significant retail footprint in underserved areas.\n\nThe label integrates with the Fairness Testing view (Task 12) so that the algorithmic fairness disclosure (Category 1) is sourced directly from the fairness metric matrix, with the AI/ML Model Risk Tier label providing the tier classification. It also integrates with the Consumer Duty view (Task 12) for Category 2, with the Compliance Manual for the workforce diversity policy (Category 3), and with the Localization Matrix view for jurisdiction-specific community investment requirements (e.g. U.S. Community Reinvestment Act, U.K. Financial Inclusion Strategy).',
    },
